\section*{I. INTRODUCTION}

There is no denying that accurate modeling of the \(1 / r\)-decaying long-range electrostatics is crucial in atomistic simulations, particularly for interfaces, charge-transfer processes, polar and ionic materials, and biomolecular systems. \({ }^{1-3}\) The open question has always been how "easy" it is to incorporate such long-range interactions into the machine learning interatomic potential (MLIP) framework.

Most MLIPs are built on the locality assumption: \({ }^{4}\) the energy and forces associated with a central atom are largely determined by its local neighbors. \({ }^{5,6}\) The total short-range energy of a system is thus approximated as a sum of local atomic energy contributions \(E_{i}\) from each atom \(i, E^{\mathrm{sr}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} E_{i}\). Early MLIPs \({ }^{7,8}\) define the local environment using a fixed spatial cutoff radius, typically on the order of 6 Å. Later message-passing neural networks (MPNNs) \({ }^{9-14}\) propagate information across multiple interaction shells, effectively increasing the receptive field. However, this is no guarantee that long-range
interactions are faithfully captured, as deep MPNNs can suffer from information degradation and over-smoothing. \({ }^{15,16}\)

In recent years, various strategies \({ }^{6,17-52}\) have been proposed to combine electrostatics with MLIPs. In Fig. 1, we illustrate the representative developments and the conceptual connections between them. This is by no means exhaustive and is skewed toward the earlier methods in each category, particularly those that have inspired our own work.

The methods in the purple shaded region of Fig. 1 capture longrange effects by embedding global geometrical information directly into the model representation. \({ }^{37-42,44,45}\) This includes approaches based on global or reciprocal-space message passing, \({ }^{38,44}\) the use of virtual global nodes for communicating global information, \({ }^{45}\) and those constructing explicit long-range descriptors. \({ }^{39-41}\) In these frameworks, long-range interactions are not introduced as a physical electrostatic energy term, but rather as part of the learned global feature space from which the neural network can infer nonlocal interactions.

