The methods inside the blue shaded region of Fig. 1 encode physically motivated quantities, such as atomic charges, \({ }^{22-25,27-29,31-34,48,49}\) typically trained on electronic information from density functional theory (DFT). Notably, 3G-HDNNP \({ }^{23,31}\) learns DFT partial charges, \({ }^{31}\) and this was later refined in 4G-HDNNP \({ }^{23}\) that further include the charge equilibration (Qeq) scheme. \({ }^{47,53}\) PhysNet, \({ }^{22}\) BAMBOO, \({ }^{25}\) and AIMNET2 \({ }^{32}\) include DFT dipoles in the loss function and predict atomic partial charges. The deep potential long-range (DPLR) model \({ }^{35}\) and the self-consistent field neural network (SCFNN) \({ }^{36}\) learn from the maximally localized Wannier centers (MLWCs) for insulating systems.

In a separate vein, the methods highlighted in the red shaded region specifically target electrical response properties, such as polarization \((P)\), \({ }^{54-61}\) polarizability, \({ }^{54,57,58}\) electron density, \({ }^{62-64}\) and Born effective charge (BEC) tensors \(\left(Z^{*}\right) .^{54-56,59-61,65}\) These quantities may be learned directly as tensorial outputs using equivariant features \({ }^{55,56,62,63,65-68}\) or through differentiable learning frameworks in which response properties are obtained as derivatives of learned quantities, \({ }^{54,60}\) for example, BECs as derivatives of polarization with respect to atomic displacements \({ }^{54,56,60}\) and polarizations \(P\) as derivatives of energy with respect to the external electric field \(\mathcal{E}\). \({ }^{54,57,58}\) Related developments by Grisafi et al. \({ }^{62,64}\) introduced long-range
symmetry-adapted models for predicting electron densities and the electronic density response under applied fields.

Latent Ewald Summation (LES) \({ }^{21,59-61}\) is at the intersection of the three aforementioned approaches: LES explicitly employs atomic charges to construct a physical long-range Coulomb electrostatic energy term; however, these charges are not learned from DFT electronic labels. Instead, they are inferred as latent, environmentdependent features solely from training on standard energy and force data. Meanwhile, electrical response properties such as \(P\) and \(Z^{*}\) emerge naturally. \({ }^{59-61}\)

The LES algorithm is simple: the local invariant features on each atom \(i\) (same as the ones for predicting local energies \(E_{i}\) ) predict the latent atomic charge \(q^{\text {les }}\) via a neural network. These \(q^{\text {les }}\) values are then used to compute long-range electrostatic energy \(E^{\text {lr }}\). Pairwise summation is used for finite systems,
\[
E^{\mathrm{lr}}=\frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \sum_{j=1}^{N}\left[1-\varphi\left(r_{i j}\right)\right] \frac{q_{i}^{\text {les }} q_{j}^{\text {les }}}{r_{i j}},
\]
where \(\varepsilon_{0}\) is the vacuum permittivity, \(\varphi(r)=\operatorname{erfc}\left(\frac{r}{\sqrt{2} \sigma}\right)\) is the complementary error function, and \(\sigma\) is a smearing factor typically \(\approx 1 \AA\). It is worth noting that for moderately large molecules, it may still be
computationally advantageous to setup a periodic box with sufficient vacuum space. Ewald summation is used for periodic systems,
\[
E^{\operatorname{lr}}=\frac{1}{2 \varepsilon_{0} V} \sum_{0<k<k_{c}} \frac{1}{k^{2}} e^{-\sigma^{2} k^{2} / 2}|S(\mathbf{k})|^{2}
\]
with
\[
S(\mathbf{k})=\sum_{i=1}^{N} q_{i}^{\mathrm{les}} e^{i \mathbf{k} \cdot \mathbf{r}_{i}}
\]
where \(\mathbf{k}\) is the reciprocal wave vector and \(V\) is the cell volume. Finally, the electrostatic energy is added to the short-range energy to form the total potential energy of the system, \(E=E^{\mathrm{sr}}+E^{\mathrm{lr}}\).

For a finite system, the polarization is \(P=\sum_{i=1}^{N} q_{i} \mathbf{r}_{i}\) and the BEC for an atom \(i\) is
\[
Z_{i \alpha \beta}^{*}=\frac{\partial P_{\alpha}}{\partial r_{i \beta}}
\]
with \(\alpha\) and \(\beta\) labeling Cartesian directions. For a homogeneous periodic system,
\[
Z_{i \alpha \beta}^{*}=\lim _{k \rightarrow 0} \Re\left[\exp \left(-i k r_{i \alpha}\right) \frac{\partial P_{\alpha}(k)}{\partial r_{i \beta}}\right],
\]
where \(P_{\alpha}(k)=\sum_{i=1}^{N} \sqrt{\varepsilon_{\infty}} \frac{q_{i}^{\text {les }}}{i k} \exp \left(i k r_{i \alpha}\right)\) and \(\varepsilon_{\infty}\) is high-frequency (electronic) relative permittivity.

In addition to the simple algorithm, the implementation of LES into any short-range MLIP is also easy. Thus far, LES has been integrated with MACE, \({ }^{12}\) NequIP, \({ }^{11}\) Allegro, \({ }^{69}\) CACE, \({ }^{70}\) and CHGNet, \({ }^{71}\) and these implementations are discussed and benchmarked in

